Z||000029||Camaro_hunter_d||08-30-2001||07:23 PM||||how turbo's work.  <P>  Turbos have 2 turbines 1 on intake and 1 on exhaust. The exhaust gasses pass over the exhaust turbine(which is smaller then the intake turbine) and spins the connecting "pin axle" to the intake turbine. The intake turbine spins at the same rate as the exhaust turbine and draws in air. Turbo lag is defined as the time it take for a turbo to spool up. The smaller the exhaust turbine the faster it spools up BUT the faster it peaks on its max RPM's or Boost.<P><BR>  Most of todays turbo's are quite reliable and extremely dependable. The biggest problem that people run into with burning up turbo's is "cooking the turbo", which is after running around town for a while simply turning off the engine and letting the oil rest where it will. THIS IS NOT A GOOD THING. If you have a turbo YOU NEED A TURBO TIMER TO CONTROL THE OIL TEMP. If you shut the engine down with out the oil being semi cool(engine temp below 160ish) you will cook the turbo and burn the bearings out.<BR>  Turbo timers work by keeping the cars engine running for approx 5 minutes to allow the oil in the turbo to cool off, hense cooling of the internal turbo components. Timers are a good investment at around 50-100 bucks a pop. Don't worry about the car walking of or being stolen. If you have a alarm it will kill the engine imediately, or you can wire in a kill switch override. <BR>  The other biggest problem turbo owners run into is oil supply itself. Most turbos do not get enough oil to keep it happy and lubed up. Remember that turbo RPM's run into the 125k range and then some. The other thing you have to keep tabs on is the valvetrain components. Remember these few things and you and your turbo will ive a long and happy life.<P>   ||||reg||1
Z||000030||sluppy123||08-30-2001||11:18 PM||||Mach10- I had to relocate the vapor canister,I moved it to the right side of the engine compartment, where the battery was, moved battery up front under spare tire. The Vacuum canister under the cruise control acuator, went behind the driver side quarter panel. I also moved the oil pressure sensor, it's now mounted on top the turbo. <P>If you want I can take some pics for ya.<BR><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>               <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||BV MotorSports||08-31-2001||01:29 AM||||Have you installed an oil temp guage yet? The reason I ask is that any oil temp over 260 and bye bye engine. I am into turbo cars and have owned quite a few over the yrs. <BR>You may want to hit the local j-yard and pick up an oil cooler from a Mitsubishi Starion or Chrysler Conquest just to be on the safe side.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||4
Z||000032||Turbo88||08-31-2001||01:43 AM||||Anyone looking for new/reman units.. I have a place that I can get them cheap. Some consulting work paid for my twins (Garrett T3s) but I talked with them about getting a discount on turbo's and they agreed.. (I got another one to turbo my wife's prizm.. I LOVE TURBOS!!!)<P>Anyway, if anyone wants anything.. give me the details (engine specs and HP targets) and I'll work up the numbers for the right turbo. If you already know what you want, let me know and I'll run it by them. These guys also gave me a couple old diesel turbos for a jet engine project <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P><BR>bryan_es@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000033||pontiacs4me||08-31-2001||02:49 AM||||Turbo88,<P>I got one for you, I am having a hard time getting turbo info straight, hopefully you can help me.  I was wanting to build up a 3.4 60* v6, but I am changing my mind again.  Back to my original plan of a 88-90 3.8 90* v6.   I plan to use 8.5:1 comp.  forged pistons, larger than stock cam, ported heads, intake and custom headers.  I am looking for 330 horse at the crank, with a 6500 redline.  10-12 lbs of intercooled boost.  Haltech ECU, through a laptop, boost controlers, and ignition retard, possibly 60 shot of nitrous.  What turbo do you recommend?||||reg||1
Z||000034||87GTZ34||08-31-2001||08:09 AM||||What do you want the torque curve to look like?  ie. Do you want the turbo to assist with lower end torque or just build power in the max HP portion of the curve.  Also, is this a street beater, strip/street or pure strip car?  Answering these questions will help you in selecting the right turbo for your application.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Turbo88||08-31-2001||12:23 PM||||Ok here is what I come up with.. I believe  your motor is an L67 which is stock with 8.5:1 comp. I need some more specifics that, larger than stock cam and ported heads and intake, and custom headers. If I were building the kit, I would need to know lift/duration, how much porting will be done (bowls only, ports expanded, how much, polished?)and what type of intercooler you plan to use. Also, if you could tell me what car it came out of, that would help out. As far as I can tell there wasn't a common 3.8 in 88-90. Here is the break out as best as I can tell.... <P> CID   Years   Bore   Stroke  Block Material  Actual   Litres   Main <BR>                                               CID             Bearing <P> 200   78-79   3.50    3.48     Cast Iron      200.9    3.29   2 bolt <BR> 229   80-84   3.74    3.48     Cast Iron      229.4    3.76   2 bolt <BR> 262   85-94   4.00    3.48     Cast Iron      262.4    4.30   2 bolt <BR> 231*   96-    3.80    3.40     Cast Iron      231.4    3.79   2 bolt <P>Making a lot of assumptions, a T04b H3 with a turbine housing .68 A/R will do well for you. Again it's a ball park but I think it's very close. I wouldn't run NO2 but if you are hard set on that idea, run a 35 shot from 200 over idle to about 2500-3000 rpm. That will give you a little more kick off the line and help spool your turbo up but won't run you lean or overheat your turbine at boost. With no intercooler, a ballpark flywheel HP rating of 356hp with 10 lbs of boost. Sinse this isn't a perfect world, depending on where you live I would say 320-360 would be the range you can expect. Again, this is depending on a well designed system that matches everything up. If you have any further questions, feel free to email me or post here ( bryan_es@yahoo.com )<BR>||||reg||1
